# Mars-like body for VPLanet's ThermInt + RadHeat modules.
#
# ThermInt tracks the temperature of a mantle and a core, the heat flow
# across the core-mantle boundary, and a magnetic moment. RadHeat adds
# heat from uranium, thorium, and potassium as they decay.
#
# Stock VPLanet (the one `pip install vplanet` builds) fixes the metallic
# core radius at Earth's, 3481 km. It has no input for 1650 km, 1830 km,
# or 613 km. Those three sizes are on the recommended path,
# challenge/run_explorer_model.mjs. Treat the moment from this file as a
# practice integration. The core inside the code is Earth's core.
#
# A line that starts with # is a comment. VPLanet ignores it.
# Negative values are in Earth units: -0.107 is 0.107 Earth masses.

sName mars
saModules radheat thermint

# --- Orbit and spin. Mars relative to Earth. ---
dMass -0.10745
dRadius -0.533
dRotPeriod -1.0259567
dObliquity 25.2
dRadGyra 0.366
dEcc 0.0934
dSemi -1.5237

# --- Temperatures, in kelvin. These are the knobs for impact heat. ---
# dTMan is the average mantle temperature.
# dTCore is the average core temperature.
# dTSurf is the surface temperature.
# The core-mantle boundary temperature starts near dTCore times dAdJumpC2CMB.
#
# This file does not contain an impactor. To represent impact heat, raise
# one temperature and run again.
#   Mantle heat: try dTMan 1800 instead of 1600.
#   Core heat:   try dTCore 3200 instead of 2750.
# Write the impactor diameter you have in mind into case.json. VPLanet does
# not read that number. It only keeps the website's grid attached to this run.
dTMan 1600
dTCore 2750
dTSurf 250
dAdJumpC2CMB 0.8

# dTrefLind is the reference temperature in the core's melting curve.
# The default is Earth's, and at these Mars-like temperatures that default
# can freeze the whole core at the first step (the inner-core radius, RIC,
# jumps from 0 to thousands of km, and the moment drops to 0).
# 1300 K keeps the core liquid so you can see a magnetic history.
# It is not a measured Mars melting temperature.
# Delete this line on a second run if you want to see the freeze.
dTrefLind 1300

# --- Lid. ---
# dStagLid 0 is a mobile lid: the surface can carry heat out more easily.
# Any value above 0 is a stagnant lid: surface heat flow is cut to 1/25.
# Stagnant is the Mars-like starting point. Set this to 0 to try a weaker lid.
dStagLid 1

# Activation energy of the mantle viscosity, J/mol.
# Higher means viscosity changes more strongly with temperature.
dActViscMan 300000

# Water is not a VPLanet input. A wetter mantle is weaker, which you can
# explore by uncommenting the next line (a lower reference viscosity).
# If you do, set "water_fraction" in case.json from 0 to 1 so the website
# file records that choice. Leave the line commented on the first run.
# dViscRef 6e6

# Solar-wind pressure at Mars, in pascal. About Earth's pressure divided
# by the square of Mars's orbital distance. About 1.15 nPa.
dPresSWind 1.15e-9

# --- Radiogenic heating. ---
# A negative power is in "Earth mantle" units. -1 would be Earth's mantle
# power. -0.11 is that power times 0.11, a Mars-mass scale (Mars is about
# a tenth of Earth's mass). It is a starting scale, not a lab measurement.
# Crust and core are set to 0 so Earth's crustal default is not added on top.
#
# Try -0.05 and -0.30 on a later run. Change all four mantle lines together.
d40KPowerMan -0.11
d40KPowerCore 0
d40KPowerCrust 0
d232ThPowerMan -0.11
d232ThPowerCore 0
d232ThPowerCrust 0
d238UPowerMan -0.11
d238UPowerCore 0
d238UPowerCrust 0
d235UPowerMan -0.11
d235UPowerCore 0
d235UPowerCrust 0

# Columns in the forward file, in this order. The minus sign asks for the
# usual unit (Gyr, kelvin, W/m^2, Earth magnetic moments).
saOutputOrder -Time -TMan -TCore -TCMB -HfluxCMB -HfluxCMBAd -HfluxCMBConv -MagMom -MagPauseRad -RIC -RadPowerMan -HflowSurf
